WEBVTT

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:23.450
<v Ira Glass>As long as he could remember, going back to when he was a child, Sam was scared of spiders. But not scared in the normal way, where lots of us don't feel great when we see a big spider, or a snake, or a big bug, or whatever.
<v Ira Glass>从他记事起，大概从童年时代开始，萨姆就怕蜘蛛。但不是那种普通意义上的怕，不是我们大多数人在看到大蜘蛛、蛇、大虫子之类的东西时感到不适的那种怕。

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:42.790
<v Sam>It invaded all aspects of my life at all points in the year. I was thinking about spiders all the time. Any room I walked into, I looked in the corners. I looked under the table, crouched down. Every night before I went to bed, I fully unmade my bed.
<v Sam>它侵入了我一年四季生活的方方面面。我无时无刻不在想蜘蛛。走进任何一个房间，我都会查看角落。我会查看桌子底下，蹲下来检查。每天晚上睡觉前，我都要把床完全拆开检查一遍。

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:52.370
<v Sam>Walking down the street, I wouldn't walk under anything that-- I would try to avoid right angles to the best I could, because that's where a spider is going to make its web.
<v Sam>走在街上，我不会从任何东西下面走过——我会尽我所能避开直角，因为那是蜘蛛结网的地方。

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:56.070
<v Ira Glass>But you were scared that one would fall on you? Or just because that's where they are?
<v Ira Glass>你是怕有蜘蛛掉在你身上吗？还是仅仅因为那是它们待的地方？

00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:27.090
<v Sam>Whether or not it would fall on me was really irrelevant. Just seeing a spider, not moving, moving, large, small, it just created a feeling in my body that was just-- I would shake. I would throw up. I would faint. And of course, if you're constantly going through your life looking for spiders, you'll find one.
<v Sam>它会不会掉在我身上根本无关紧要。只要看到蜘蛛，不管它是动还是不动，是大还是小，我的身体就会产生一种感觉——我会发抖。我会呕吐。我会昏厥。当然，如果你在生活中总是试图寻找蜘蛛，你肯定会找到一只的。

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:36.620
<v Ira Glass>As a kid, he didn't do sleepovers, didn't do summer camp. Other kids made fun of him. People did not understand. People pitied him.
<v Ira Glass>小时候，他不参加过夜聚会，也不去夏令营。其他孩子取笑他。人们不理解他。人们同情他。

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:45.690
<v Ira Glass>And when he grew up, it did not go away. His fear ruined dates. He once found a spider the size of your thumbnail in his car and sold the car that day.
<v Ira Glass>当他长大成人后，这种恐惧并没有消失。他的恐惧毁了他的约会。他曾经在车里发现了一只拇指指甲大小的蜘蛛，当天就把车给卖了。

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:59.610
<v Sam>I had went to psychiatrists for exposure therapy. I had went to psychiatrists to talk about it. I couldn't watch an image of a spider on a TV screen.
<v Sam>我去看过精神科医生，进行暴露疗法。我也看过精神科医生去谈论这件事。我甚至无法在电视屏幕上看一眼蜘蛛的图像。

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.830
<v Ira Glass>Let me ask you, does this name mean anything to you?
<v Ira Glass>我问你，这个名字对你有什么意义吗？

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:02.760
<v Sam>Hmm?
<v Sam>嗯？

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.890
<v Ira Glass>Peter Parker.
<v Ira Glass>彼得·帕克（Peter Parker）。

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.200
<v Sam>No. Oh, oh, Spider Man.
<v Sam>不。哦，哦，蜘蛛侠。

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.950
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, could you watch those films?
<v Ira Glass>是的，你能看那些电影吗？

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:20.540
<v Sam>No, absolutely not. I don't even know if Spider Man has anything to do with spiders, to be honest.
<v Sam>不，绝对不行。老实说，我甚至不知道蜘蛛侠是否和蜘蛛有什么关系。

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:49.290
<v Ira Glass>And then, he was seeing a hypnotherapist, and it was going nowhere, when he read in The New York Times about this new treatment for phobias that can get full results in just one day. And he reached out to the doctor behind it, a psychologist, Dr. Merel Kindt in Amsterdam. And she invited him to be part of a study and get the treatment. He figured he had nothing to lose and flew to The Netherlands. A film crew captured what happened during his treatment for a documentary series called A Cure for Fear.
<v Ira Glass>后来，他去看了一位催眠治疗师，但毫无进展。这时他在《纽约时报》上读到了一种治疗恐惧症的新方法，只需一天就能取得全面疗效。他联系了背后的医生，一位在阿姆斯特丹的心理学家，梅雷尔·金特（Merel Kindt）博士。她邀请他参与一项研究并接受治疗。他想反正也没什么可失去的，于是飞往荷兰。一个摄制组记录了他治疗过程中发生的一切，制作成了一部名为《恐惧疗法》（A Cure for Fear）的纪录片系列。

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.370
<v Merel Kindt>You're doing fine.
<v Merel Kindt>你做得很好。

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:52.060
<v Sam>I'm so nervous.
<v Sam>我太紧张了。

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:53.170
<v Merel Kindt>Yeah, it's OK.
<v Merel Kindt>是的，没关系的。

00:02:53.170 --> 00:03:02.760
<v Ira Glass>Sam and Dr. Kindt stand outside the door of her room. She opens it. He looks in. There's an aquarium with a brown, furry tarantula, maybe 4 or 5 inches in size.
<v Ira Glass>萨姆和金特博士站在她房间的门外。她打开门。他向里看去。里面有一个水族箱，有一只棕色、毛茸茸的狼蛛，大概有4到5英寸大。

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:15.490
<v Merel Kindt>Yes, there's a spider in the tank. But let's not wait too long. So it would be very good if you can already walk in the room, and then I close the door. Very good, great. We're doing very well.
<v Merel Kindt>是的，水箱里有一只蜘蛛。但我们不要等太久。所以如果你能先走进房间，然后我关上门，那就太好了。非常好，棒极了。我们做得非常出色。

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:25.830
<v Sam>I think you can hear that I was breathing hard, and I'm feeling that there is adrenaline. I crouch down, my arms crossed.
<v Sam>我想你能听出我在剧烈呼吸，我能感觉到肾上腺素在分泌。我蹲下来，双臂交叉。

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:29.980
<v Ira Glass>Dr. Kindt then opens the door of the tank.
<v Ira Glass>接着，金特博士打开了水箱的盖子。

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:33.420
<v Sam>Oh, whoa, god. No, no, no, you're not going to make me look in there, are you?
<v Sam>哦，哇，天哪。不，不，不，你不会真的要让我往那里看吧？

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.160
<v Merel Kindt>Yes, I'm going to ask you.
<v Merel Kindt>是的，我要请你看。

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:35.370
<v Sam>[HYPERVENTILATING]
<v Sam>[过度换气]

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:40.890
<v Merel Kindt>So please come with me. So step in here, and then close it. Very good. And then, can you also--
<v Merel Kindt>所以请跟我来。站到这里，然后关上门。非常好。然后，你能也——

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:41.370
<v Sam>Oh god!
<v Sam>哦，天哪！

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:44.010
<v Merel Kindt>Very good, very good. Yeah, come. And--
<v Merel Kindt>非常好，非常好。对，过来。然后——

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.150
<v Sam>No! Don't make me go in there!
<v Sam>不！别逼我进去！

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:54.940
<v Ira Glass>Then, to get the spider to move around this tank, she sprays it with water. And every time she plays it with water, the spider waves its legs or moves around a little.
<v Ira Glass>然后，为了让蜘蛛在水箱里走动，她用水喷它。每次她用水喷它，蜘蛛就会挥动腿或者稍微移动一下。

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:55.530
<v Sam>Yeah.
<v Sam>对。

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.760
<v Merel Kindt>OK. How high is your distress right now?
<v Merel Kindt>好的。你现在的痛苦程度有多高？

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:00.000
<v Sam>It's like 100.
<v Sam>大概100分。

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.530
<v Merel Kindt>OK, but it's very important not to move away.
<v Merel Kindt>好的，但是千万不要走开，这非常重要。

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:03.510
<v Sam>OK, I'm not moving away.
<v Sam>好的，我不会走开。

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.880
<v Merel Kindt>All right, spray it a bit so that--
<v Merel Kindt>好了，喷它一下，这样——

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.660
<v Sam>(SCREAMING) Oh, god, no! [WAILS]
<v Sam>[尖叫] 哦，天哪，不！[哀号]

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:11.180
<v Merel Kindt>Yeah, that's very good. OK.
<v Merel Kindt>对，这非常好。好的。

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:14.030
<v Sam>[HYPERVENTILATING] I gotta go!
<v Sam>[过度换气] 我得走了！

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:18.360
<v Ira Glass>Sam, I'm wondering, like when you scream like this, I'm wondering what goes through your head.
<v Ira Glass>萨姆，我很好奇，当你这样尖叫时，我在想你的脑子里在想什么。

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:27.030
<v Sam>That I feel like I can feel it on me, that I'm going to be attacked by it. None of this is rational, right?
<v Sam>我觉得我能感觉到它在我身上，我觉得我要被它攻击了。这一切都不理性，对吧？

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:27.440
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hmm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:28.450
<v Sam>I know it's not--
<v Sam>我知道它不是——

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:49.580
<v Sam>I know that the thing isn't going to jump out of the tank and move like 4 feet in the air and jump on me. I get that. But it doesn't matter, because I feel that the absolute worst things that can happen are going to happen and are, in fact, happening.
<v Sam>我知道那东西不会从水箱里跳出来，在空中飞4英尺扑到我身上。我明白。但那没关系，因为我觉得绝对最糟糕的事情即将发生，而且实际上正在发生。

00:04:49.580 --> 00:05:23.220
<v Ira Glass>The reason Dr. Kent wants him to max out on anxiety like this is that she wants to trigger the memories and feelings of fear of spiders that are stored in his brain. And then, when his brain goes to store this big new terrible experience with the old ones, it has to re-save the old memories. And she gives him a drug, a beta blocker called propranolol, that disrupts that process. And I know this sounds so simple. How can this be real? But by disrupting the way that the brain re-saves those memories, she neutralizes them.
<v Ira Glass>金特博士想让他焦虑达到极限的原因是，她想触发储存在他大脑中对蜘蛛的记忆和恐惧感。然后，当他的大脑将这种巨大的、可怕的新体验与旧的记忆存储在一起时，它就必须重新保存旧的记忆。她给了他一种药物，一种叫做普萘洛尔（propranolol）的β受体阻滞剂，可以中断这个过程。我知道这听起来很简单。这怎么可能是真的呢？但通过破坏大脑重新保存这些记忆的方式，她中和了它们。

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:29.290
<v Ira Glass>The very next day, Sam returns to the same room. He walks right in. His breathing is normal.
<v Ira Glass>第二天，萨姆回到了同一个房间。他直接走了进去。呼吸正常。

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:38.380
<v Sam>There's fear in that-- well, I don't know that there's fear. I don't understand my feeling, because I've never been like this before.
<v Sam>这里面有恐惧——嗯，我不知道是否有恐惧。我不理解我的感觉，因为我以前从来没有这样过。

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:44.050
<v Sam>Nothing physically, internally, was happening that used to happen to.
<v Sam>身体内部没有发生任何过去常发生的事情。

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:45.550
<v Ira Glass>You didn't feel the fear?
<v Ira Glass>你没有感觉到恐惧吗？

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:47.030
<v Sam>I didn't feel the fear. And when she said--
<v Sam>我没有感觉到恐惧。当她说——

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:48.100
<v Ira Glass>No adrenaline?
<v Ira Glass>没有肾上腺素分泌？

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:53.340
<v Sam>No adrenaline at all. I felt, I guess, excitement that this was new.
<v Sam>完全没有肾上腺素。我想，我感到的是一种新鲜感。

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:54.940
<v Merel Kindt>You think you are able to touch it?
<v Merel Kindt>你觉得你能摸它吗？

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:56.510
<v Sam>And she says, do you want to pet it?
<v Sam>她说，你想摸摸它吗？

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:57.410
<v Merel Kindt>Let's make a deal.
<v Merel Kindt>我们做个交易吧。

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:09.670
<v Sam>And I said, look, if you hold my hand, I'll pet the spider. And I did. And I petted it, and the thing started to move. And then she's like, do you want to pet it again? And I said, yes.
<v Sam>我说，你看，如果你握着我的手，我就摸那只蜘蛛。我做了。我摸了它，那东西开始动了。然后她说，你想再摸一次吗？我说，是的。

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:11.860
<v Merel Kindt>You touched the tarantula!
<v Merel Kindt>你摸了那只狼蛛！

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:50.110
<v Ira Glass>That was over three years ago, and Sam says the effect of the treatment has only deepened as he's had more calm interactions with spiders. He doesn't look around for spiders anymore everywhere he goes. And remember he once sold a car when he found a spider in it? He told me that the day before I interviewed him, he was driving in the SUV that he owns today, flipped down the mirror, a spider dropped into his lap. He was going 60. He said if that had happened back in the day, before the treatment, he really might have crashed his car. But now, he rolled down the window, picked up the spider, threw it outside.
<v Ira Glass>那是三年多以前的事了，萨姆说，随着他与蜘蛛的互动变得更加平静，治疗的效果反而加深了。他再也不会去任何地方都查看有没有蜘蛛了。还记得他曾经因为在车里发现蜘蛛就把车卖了吗？他告诉我，在我采访他的前一天，他开着现在拥有的那辆SUV，翻下遮阳板时，一只蜘蛛掉进了他的膝盖上。他当时车速有60迈。他说，如果是在以前，治疗前，他真的可能会把车撞毁。但现在，他摇下车窗，捡起蜘蛛，把它扔到了外面。

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:53.320
<v Ira Glass>Can you talk about just, like, how extreme the treatment was?
<v Ira Glass>你能谈谈这次治疗有多极端吗？

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:59.320
<v Sam>I thought at one point I was going to have a heart attack and die in that room. [LAUGHS] Yeah.
<v Sam>我有那么一刻以为自己会心脏病发作死在那个房间里。[笑] 是的。

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:07.430
<v Ira Glass>Does it seem right somehow that you would have to go through something so extreme to get over something so extreme?
<v Ira Glass>为了克服极端的事物而必须经历如此极端的事情，这听起来合理吗？

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:12.380
<v Sam>It's what had to be done, fighting fire with fire.
<v Sam>这是必须要做的事，以毒攻毒。

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:12.750
<v Sam>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Sam>[音乐播放]

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:28.050
<v Sam>Radiation therapy, it's your last resort option that you have to deal with a problem that can't be solved in any other way. This is the nuclear option.
<v Sam>放射疗法，这是你处理无法以其他方式解决的问题时的最后手段。这是“核选项”。

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:59.910
<v Ira Glass>Well, today, our program, the nuclear option. We have stories where there's a problem, somebody decides to throw the book at it, go big, do everything they can think of. And we see what happens as a result to the people that the flying book is aimed at, but also the people it is not aimed at who get hit with it anyway. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm your friendly neighborhood radio host, Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>那么，今天我们的节目就是“核选项”。我们会讲一些故事，当遇到问题时，有人决定全力以赴，孤注一掷，尽其所能。我们要看看结果如何——不仅是对那些被“飞书”瞄准的人，也对那些即使没被瞄准也难免遭殃的人。来自芝加哥WBEZ广播电台，这里是《美国生活》（This American Life）。我是你们友好的邻家广播主持人艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:30.530
<v Ira Glass>Act 1, "Catching Deer, When You Wanted Lions." So we begin with an example of the United States Congress seeing a problem and then using all its powers, doing everything it can, moving decisively to eliminate the problem forever. The problem they were solving in this case? Well, prostitution is illegal in America, but there were millions of ads for it online, some of them advertising women and children who were held by sex traffickers. Congress wanted to stop the sex traffickers' ads.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“捕捉鹿，却想要狮子。”我们先从一个例子开始：美国国会发现了一个问题，然后利用其所有权力，尽其所能，果断行动以彻底消除该问题。他们在这个案例中要解决的问题是什么？嗯，卖淫在美国是非法的，但网上有数百万条相关广告，其中一些广告招募的是被性贩运者控制的妇女和儿童。国会想要取缔性贩运者的广告。

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:49.160
<v Ira Glass>So that's a goal I think most people would agree with. A law passes with overwhelming majorities, House and Senate. One of our producers, Lina Misitzis has been looking into how things played out from that point-- with the people that the law is supposed to help, victims of sex trafficking, but also with other people who are not the official target of the law.
<v Ira Glass>我认为这是一个大多数人都会赞同的目标。这项法律在众议院和参议院以压倒性多数通过。我们的制片人之一琳娜·米西齐斯（Lina Misitzis）一直在观察从那时起事情是如何发展的——不仅是对法律本应帮助的人，即性贩运受害者，还有那些并非该法律正式目标的人。

00:08:49.160 --> 00:09:06.280
<v Ira Glass>We're going to hear the story of one woman who was trafficked, and then you're going to hear the story of the law, and then, after that, the story of how the law actually affected her. Warning to listeners-- there is no explicit content in this story at all, but it does talk about sex and sex trafficking. Here's Lina.
<v Ira Glass>我们要听的是一个曾经被贩运的女性的故事，然后你会听到关于这项法律的故事，之后是这项法律如何实际影响了她的故事。提醒听众——这个故事中没有任何露骨的内容，但确实讨论了性和性贩运。这是琳娜的报道。

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:31.640
<v Lina Misitzis>When people hear the phrase "sex trafficking," I think a lot of the time, a kind of cartoonish version of it pops up in their heads. Let me tell you what it meant in Kara's case. We'll start in 2006 when she was 17, a runaway in Orlando working at a pizza place. She decided to try becoming a stripper, figuring it would be better money, but she really didn't know anything about it. She had no idea what she was doing.
<v Lina Misitzis>当人们听到“性贩运”这个词时，我想大多数时候，脑海中浮现的是一种卡通般的版本。让我告诉你在卡拉（Kara）的案例中这意味着什么。我们从2006年开始，当时她17岁，是奥兰多一家披萨店的离家出走者。她决定尝试成为一名脱衣舞娘，觉得赚钱会更多，但她对这一行一无所知。她根本不知道自己在做什么。

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:36.280
<v Lina Misitzis>To prepare for her first shift she went dress shopping, found a prom dress she liked.
<v Lina Misitzis>为了准备第一次轮班，她去买衣服，挑了一件她喜欢的舞会礼服。

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:41.030
<v Kara>Yeah, it was a purple dress. I got it from Macy's.
<v Kara>是的，那是一件紫色的裙子。我是在梅西百货买的。

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:42.280
<v Lina Misitzis>Was it like floor length?
<v Lina Misitzis>是落地长裙吗？

00:09:42.280 --> 00:10:13.340
<v Kara>Yeah, it was floor length, and it came right over my knees. It was a halter top. And I'm just looking, and I see all these girls, and they're in fishnets and clear heels. And this was about the time there was a song. It's called "Apple-Bottom Jeans," [LAUGHS] the boots with the fur. So I kind of just did what I could and jumped on a pole, and I slid down. But it wasn't like a sexy stripper slide. It was like holding on for dear life and kind of bouncing down. [LAUGHS]
